WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.380
MachinePulseAI turns measurement data into
engineering insight.

2
00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:13.980
Prepare data, analyze signals and structures,
and report in one environment.

3
00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:19.440
Test and R&D teams can inspect recordings and
share the results in a report.

4
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.460
In MachinePulseAI, the whole workflow from
data to report shares one workspace.

5
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:29.260
Data I/O opens, combines and exports
measurement files.

6
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.940
Preprocessing organizes the data; filters
prepare the signal.

7
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.360
Engineering brings frequency, vibration, rotor
and structural analyses together.

8
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:44.376
Thermal and fluids, materials and strength,
electrical, acoustics, control and vehicle

9
00:00:44.376 --> 00:00:46.840
dynamics are planned for later phases.

10
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:54.580
Statistics supports comparison and monitoring.
Machine Learning is planned for later phases.

11
00:00:54.580 --> 00:01:02.200
Inspect results in Visualization, share them
in Reporting, and add custom Python steps.

12
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:09.393
Tools from each available group form a
workflow. Connect their ports into

13
00:01:09.393 --> 00:01:13.860
preparation, analysis, plot and report
branches.

14
00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.920
Activity shows operation status and diagnostic
logs.

15
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.060
Inspector shows the selected widget's status
and connections.

16
00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:28.240
Use the AI assistant when you need it, or
manage the workflow yourself.

17
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:37.280
Ask explains methods and queries data
read-only, preserving your workflow.

18
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:45.526
With a compatible model, use the app through
prompts in optional Agent mode. It can connect

19
00:01:45.526 --> 00:01:48.940
tools, run analyses and prepare reports.

20
00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:56.620
Choose supported cloud services or compatible
local models.

21
00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:06.873
Run compatible models on your own computer for
sensitive data. Local Only Mode blocks cloud

22
00:02:06.873 --> 00:02:12.720
providers, keeping measurements out of those
services.

23
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.680
Data I/O contains five tools available in the
first phase.

24
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:23.560
File Reader opens measurements and shows their
channels and recording details.

25
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:30.760
Geometry Source links measurement points to a
structure for inspecting analysis results.

26
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.880
File Merger combines recording parts into one
data source.

27
00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:42.060
File Exporter writes selected channels to
files for use in other applications.

28
00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:48.060
Data Generator creates controlled test signals
without a measurement file.

29
00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:53.020
Let's open these tools and see how they work
in short examples.

30
00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:59.660
File Reader shows the selected file's channels
and recording details.

31
00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:05.800
The file is ready in the workspace and can
feed different tools.

32
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:11.080
Geometry Source builds a simple beam geometry.

33
00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.700
The resulting geometry is available for
structural analyses.

34
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.160
File Merger combines recording parts into one
dataset.

35
00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:26.540
The parts now form one source, with the chosen
time-axis policy.

36
00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.560
File Exporter lets us choose a channel and
output format.

37
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:38.880
The selected channel was exported to CSV for
use in other applications.

38
00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.840
Data Generator creates test signals without a
measurement file.

39
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:50.360
The new channel appears immediately and can
feed other widgets.

40
00:03:50.633 --> 00:03:55.313
Preprocessing organizes, scales and prepares
data for analysis.

41
00:03:55.973 --> 00:04:00.773
Channel Selector passes only the channels
needed by the next steps.

42
00:04:01.293 --> 00:04:06.653
Dataset Merge combines separate sources by
rows or channels.

43
00:04:07.093 --> 00:04:13.433
Data Table Editor supports cell editing,
formulas and other operations within the app.

44
00:04:13.873 --> 00:04:19.553
Normalization scales channels to a common
basis for comparison.

45
00:04:19.553 --> 00:04:25.613
Group By summarizes data and computes
aggregate statistics using selected rules.

46
00:04:26.093 --> 00:04:30.573
Time Alignment brings separate recordings onto
a shared time axis.

47
00:04:30.873 --> 00:04:35.893
Rolling statistics show how signal level
changes throughout a recording.

48
00:04:36.373 --> 00:04:42.273
Sensor Calibration converts measured values
into engineering units.

49
00:04:42.933 --> 00:04:47.933
Let's look inside two examples: Data Table
Editor and Time Alignment.

50
00:04:47.933 --> 00:04:55.013
Data Table Editor edits and cleans data within
the app.

51
00:04:55.133 --> 00:05:00.193
Row operations, cleaning and resampling share
this window.

52
00:05:00.513 --> 00:05:06.283
In Formula, we multiply the channel by two.
Its name and expression are visible.

53
00:05:06.893 --> 00:05:11.083
The new channel is in the table and ready for
downstream analyses.

54
00:05:11.973 --> 00:05:18.893
Time Alignment compares two recordings with
different start times.

55
00:05:19.173 --> 00:05:24.973
We shift the second recording left, bringing
the same event to the same time.

56
00:05:25.193 --> 00:05:28.953
The aligned channels are now available
together in one dataset.

57
00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:35.587
Signal Filters reduces noise, transforms
signals and cleans data.

58
00:05:35.727 --> 00:05:40.387
Frequency Filter keeps or suppresses a
selected frequency band.

59
00:05:40.907 --> 00:05:47.027
Smoothing Filter reduces rapid fluctuations to
reveal signal trends.

60
00:05:47.587 --> 00:05:53.387
Math & Calculus applies absolute value,
differentiation and integration to channels.

61
00:05:53.387 --> 00:05:59.787
Advanced Diagnostics uses envelope analysis to
reveal vibration modulation.

62
00:06:00.227 --> 00:06:04.987
Kalman Filter estimates a more stable signal
from noisy measurements.

63
00:06:05.547 --> 00:06:10.927
Spike Detection/Remove finds brief spikes and
cleans them with the chosen method.

64
00:06:11.447 --> 00:06:19.047
Outlier Detection/Remove flags unusual samples
or replaces them with missing values.

65
00:06:19.047 --> 00:06:24.967
Range Filter keeps rows matching selected
conditions in a new dataset.

66
00:06:25.587 --> 00:06:29.467
Let's open all eight tools and look at short
examples.

67
00:06:30.087 --> 00:06:36.927
Frequency Filter reduces high-frequency
fluctuations with a low-pass filter.

68
00:06:37.207 --> 00:06:41.207
The raw signal and new filtered channel share
the same graph.

69
00:06:41.967 --> 00:06:46.887
Smoothing Filter smooths the signal while
retaining its overall shape.

70
00:06:46.887 --> 00:06:51.567
Showing both curves makes the smoothing effect
clear.

71
00:06:51.987 --> 00:06:57.207
Math & Calculus creates an absolute-value
channel.

72
00:06:57.687 --> 00:07:01.507
Negative portions of the raw signal become
positive in the new channel.

73
00:07:02.067 --> 00:07:07.607
Advanced Diagnostics extracts the vibration
envelope.

74
00:07:08.067 --> 00:07:11.807
The new envelope channel is visible alongside
the raw vibration.

75
00:07:11.807 --> 00:07:16.887
Kalman Filter estimates the signal with less
measurement noise.

76
00:07:17.087 --> 00:07:21.807
The raw channel is preserved for comparison
with the filtered estimate.

77
00:07:22.167 --> 00:07:28.207
Spike Detection/Remove finds spikes and
creates a cleaned channel.

78
00:07:28.467 --> 00:07:32.947
The raw and cleaned signals show which samples
changed.

79
00:07:33.307 --> 00:07:38.947
Outlier Detection/Remove flags outlying
samples in a new channel.

80
00:07:39.347 --> 00:07:43.447
The flag channel is in the table and raw
measurements are preserved.

81
00:07:43.547 --> 00:07:48.187
Range Filter keeps rows where the signal is at
or above zero.

82
00:07:48.507 --> 00:07:54.367
Matching rows form a new dataset that can feed
downstream steps.

83
00:07:54.733 --> 00:07:59.833
Engineering starts with spectra and
relationships between channels.

84
00:07:59.913 --> 00:08:03.173
We compare channel spectra.

85
00:08:03.473 --> 00:08:06.513
We compare input and response.

86
00:08:06.873 --> 00:08:10.253
Let's open these two tools for short examples.

87
00:08:10.993 --> 00:08:16.693
FFT Spectrum Analysis: We compare channel
spectra.

88
00:08:17.113 --> 00:08:22.233
Let's switch to a single cursor and precompute
FFT blocks across the selected range.

89
00:08:22.233 --> 00:08:26.053
Moving the cursor brings up the spectrum for
that moment.

90
00:08:26.553 --> 00:08:32.773
Later, the sweep peak moves higher. The cursor
reuses computed blocks.

91
00:08:33.293 --> 00:08:35.573
We can also inspect the phase of the same
block.

92
00:08:35.873 --> 00:08:38.153
Let's add a marker to the selected peak.

93
00:08:38.533 --> 00:08:43.273
In the time-frequency view, the sweep appears
as a rising trace.

94
00:08:43.673 --> 00:08:51.513
Spectrum, phase and time-frequency views share
the same recording.

95
00:08:51.513 --> 00:08:58.593
Frequency Response & Cross Spectrum: We
compare input and response.

96
00:08:58.593 --> 00:09:04.213
Amplitude, phase and coherence reveal the
relationship between channels.

97
00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:10.400
This group examines rotating machinery and
structural motion.

98
00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:14.240
We inspect rotor motion.

99
00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.700
We solve a single-plane balance.

100
00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:22.280
We track vibration against speed.

101
00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:26.300
We inspect encoder speed variation.

102
00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:30.700
We inspect structural modes.

103
00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:34.780
We use response-only measurements.

104
00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.600
We show motion on the structure.

105
00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:42.260
We inspect shock response.

106
00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:46.580
We inspect bearing and gear evidence.

107
00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:50.760
We inspect linked-point motion.

108
00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:54.280
We correct cross-axis effects.

109
00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:58.820
Let's inspect the important views in short
examples.

110
00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:04.360
Rotor Dynamics: We inspect rotor motion.

111
00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:10.240
Orbit, center and diagnostic views show
different aspects of rotor motion.

112
00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:16.680
Rotor Balancing: We solve a single-plane
balance.

113
00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:23.380
The polar view and measurements show the
correction and verification.

114
00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:28.800
Order Tracking: We track vibration against
speed.

115
00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:35.520
Map, trends and cursor cuts reveal orders and
resonance together.

116
00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:42.600
Torsional & Rotational Vibration: We inspect
encoder speed variation.

117
00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:48.940
Time, spectrum and order trends provide
complementary views.

118
00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:56.060
Advanced Modal Analysis: We inspect structural
modes.

119
00:10:56.060 --> 00:11:01.560
The selected poles produce a populated modal
identification table.

120
00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:07.280
Frequency response, Nyquist and identification
share the same measurement.

121
00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:14.920
Operational Modal Analysis (OMA): We use
response-only measurements.

122
00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:20.500
Stabilization, modal parameters and mode
similarity can be reviewed together.

123
00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:26.600
Time-Based ODS: We show motion on the
structure.

124
00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:32.760
Shape, time and phase help locate larger
motion.

125
00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:38.620
Shock & SRS: We inspect shock response.

126
00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:44.960
Spectrum, signed responses and nomogram
describe the same shock.

127
00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:52.060
Bearing & Gear Fault Diagnosis: We inspect
bearing and gear evidence.

128
00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:59.640
Diagnosis, kurtogram and trend show candidate
frequencies and evidence.

129
00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:06.980
Kinematic Chain (6-DOF): We inspect
linked-point motion.

130
00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:13.740
3D motion, displacement and RMS trend describe
the same motion.

131
00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:19.840
Cross-Axis Compensation: We correct cross-axis
effects.

132
00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:27.020
Time, matrix and metrics compare raw and
corrected measurements.

133
00:12:27.433 --> 00:12:33.353
Statistics summarizes data, compares
relationships and monitors changes.

134
00:12:33.613 --> 00:12:37.753
We inspect channel statistics.

135
00:12:38.133 --> 00:12:40.433
We inspect channel relationships.

136
00:12:40.973 --> 00:12:43.833
We inspect events and operating states.

137
00:12:44.333 --> 00:12:47.033
We compare measurements with a reference.

138
00:12:47.533 --> 00:12:50.693
We extract signal features.

139
00:12:51.193 --> 00:12:54.133
We monitor several channels together.

140
00:12:54.133 --> 00:12:57.613
Let's see how these tools present their
results.

141
00:12:58.213 --> 00:13:04.313
Channel Profile: We inspect channel
statistics.

142
00:13:04.553 --> 00:13:10.533
Summary, quality and robust statistics
describe the recording.

143
00:13:11.033 --> 00:13:15.733
Relationship Explorer: We inspect channel
relationships.

144
00:13:16.253 --> 00:13:21.133
The results show which channels vary together
and how strongly.

145
00:13:21.133 --> 00:13:26.673
Events & Regimes: We inspect events and
operating states.

146
00:13:27.093 --> 00:13:31.753
The event list and map locate changes in the
recording.

147
00:13:32.253 --> 00:13:37.113
Compare & Validate: We compare measurements
with a reference.

148
00:13:37.473 --> 00:13:42.513
Summary and results present the comparison
metrics.

149
00:13:42.893 --> 00:13:48.093
Feature Designer: We extract signal features.

150
00:13:48.093 --> 00:13:53.793
Features and ranking help identify useful
measures.

151
00:13:54.253 --> 00:13:59.633
Multivariate Monitor: We monitor several
channels together.

152
00:13:59.813 --> 00:14:06.253
Model, scores and contributions help explain
departures from normal behavior.

153
00:14:06.733 --> 00:14:11.253
Visualization provides different views of the
same data.

154
00:14:11.313 --> 00:14:14.913
We combine several plot types.

155
00:14:15.293 --> 00:14:18.113
We compare time channels.

156
00:14:18.713 --> 00:14:21.973
We inspect value distributions.

157
00:14:22.413 --> 00:14:25.593
We compare two channels as points.

158
00:14:25.933 --> 00:14:28.633
We compare channels by one measure.

159
00:14:29.193 --> 00:14:31.953
We compare channel distributions.

160
00:14:32.513 --> 00:14:35.813
We inspect several time channels.

161
00:14:35.813 --> 00:14:39.833
We overlay a channel and reference.

162
00:14:40.293 --> 00:14:44.373
We inspect a computed spectrum.

163
00:14:44.453 --> 00:14:47.573
We inspect speed and order together.

164
00:14:48.393 --> 00:14:50.753
Let's open short examples of these views.

165
00:14:51.373 --> 00:14:56.573
Chart Dashboard: We combine several plot
types.

166
00:14:56.913 --> 00:15:02.453
Time, distribution and comparison plots share
one data source.

167
00:15:02.453 --> 00:15:07.253
Line Chart: We compare time channels.

168
00:15:07.673 --> 00:15:11.933
The navigator focuses the graph on a short
interval.

169
00:15:12.293 --> 00:15:17.133
Histogram: We inspect value distributions.

170
00:15:17.473 --> 00:15:21.993
The view shows the center, spread and extremes
of the distribution.

171
00:15:22.593 --> 00:15:27.273
Scatter Plot: We compare two channels as
points.

172
00:15:27.273 --> 00:15:33.033
The point pattern reveals linear and nonlinear
relationships.

173
00:15:33.433 --> 00:15:37.653
Bar Plot: We compare channels by one measure.

174
00:15:38.093 --> 00:15:42.053
Bars make differences in channel levels easy
to see.

175
00:15:42.553 --> 00:15:46.733
Box Plot: We compare channel distributions.

176
00:15:47.173 --> 00:15:52.833
Median, spread and extremes reveal differences
beyond the mean.

177
00:15:52.833 --> 00:15:58.273
Time Graphics: We inspect several time
channels.

178
00:15:58.493 --> 00:16:03.073
A short time window reveals waveform detail.

179
00:16:03.413 --> 00:16:08.753
Align / Compare: We overlay a channel and
reference.

180
00:16:09.213 --> 00:16:13.513
Overlay and difference views show where the
channels diverge.

181
00:16:13.973 --> 00:16:19.553
Frequency Domain: We inspect a computed
spectrum.

182
00:16:19.553 --> 00:16:24.593
The view reuses the existing analysis result
for closer inspection.

183
00:16:24.793 --> 00:16:31.613
Order Map View (2D / VTK 3D): We inspect speed
and order together.

184
00:16:31.993 --> 00:16:35.613
The order map also has a 3D waterfall view.

185
00:16:35.913 --> 00:16:40.933
The 2D and 3D views help track components as
speed changes.

186
00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:46.260
Reporting brings prepared results into a
shareable document.

187
00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:50.120
We combine results in a report.

188
00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:54.080
Let's prepare a report and open the exported
file.

189
00:16:54.580 --> 00:17:00.120
Intelligent Report: We combine results in a
report.

190
00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:07.100
Let's open the exported PDF with its own
plots, explanations and page layout.

191
00:17:07.100 --> 00:17:14.620
These are the exported PDF's own pages,
containing plots and explanations.

192
00:17:15.033 --> 00:17:20.813
Python Script: We add a short engine query.

193
00:17:20.813 --> 00:17:26.613
The data engine computed the result; the
script coordinated the query.
